  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>[</mo><mi>Co</mi><mo>(</mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mo>)</mo><mn>6</mn></msub><msup><mo>]</mo><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Co</mi></math> is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>+</mo><mn>3</mn></math> oxidation state with the configuration&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>6</mn></msup></math>. In the presence of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>&nbsp;a strong field ligand, the 3d electrons undergo pairing and&nbsp;&nbsp;leaving two d-orbitals empty. Hence, the hybridisation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mi>sp</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></math>&nbsp;which forms&nbsp;an inner orbital octahedral complex. The complex is diamagnetic as it has no unpaired electrons.</p><p><img src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/pyq/jee_main/cc6d985b-aa87-4e2e-a4f0-4807ba6f95c8-9894226.png\" style=\"width: 600px; height: 262px;\" /></p><p>So, the correct option is C</p>",
